// qbrender.nv — the statement out as bytes, with the parameters
// beside them.
//
// NOTHING HERE WRITES.  Every function takes the buffer to append to
// and answers it back, so a caller building a batch of statements
// keeps one buffer and a test asserts on bytes with no database in the
// room.  `docs/publishing.md` § Design is explicit that a library
// returns the text or takes the writer, and this package has no
// effects with which to do otherwise.
//
// THE PARAMETER LIST COMES OUT, NEVER IN.  `render` walks the tree and
// assigns a position to each `QbBind` as it meets it, in the order the
// dialect's placeholders appear, and answers the list beside the
// bytes.  A caller cannot get the two out of step because it never
// wrote either.  This is the whole point of the package and every
// signature here is arranged around it.
//
// THE RENDER IS THE VALIDATOR.  There is no separate `validate` pass:
// the walk that emits the bytes is the walk that finds the join with
// no `ON`, and a statement that renders is a statement that was well
// formed.  `check` is the same walk stopping short of the buffer, for
// a caller that wants every fault rather than the first — because
// `render` stops at the first, which is what a caller executing a
// query wants, and a linter wants the other thing.
//
// EVERY DIALECT DIFFERENCE IS A `qbdialect` CALL.  There is no `match
// d` in this module's design beyond what the table cannot express;
// see that module's head comment for why.
//
// THE BYTES END WITH A ZERO.  `render`, `render_expr` and
// `write_literal` append one zero byte after the statement, because
// the `Str` they are usually turned into is NUL-terminated and the
// drivers take one; `render_str` strips it again rather than letting
// it ride into the `sql` a caller prints.  `write_ident` does not,
// because the statement walk calls it in the middle of a statement.
// `render_len` counts the byte, so its answer is `list.len` of what
// `render` appends.

use std.bytes
use std.list
use std.str

use qbast
use qbdialect
use qberror

// What a render is allowed to do and what it is bounded by.
pub struct QbRenderOptions
    // Which SQL to write.
    dialect: qbdialect.QbDialect
    // Write newlines and indentation between clauses.  False writes
    // one line, which is what goes to a server; true is what goes in a
    // log a person reads.
    pretty: Bool
    // Quote every identifier, rather than only the ones that need it.
    // False is the default and produces readable SQL; true is what a
    // caller whose column names came from a schema they do not control
    // wants.  A dialect that cannot quote refuses this rather than
    // ignoring it.
    quote_all: Bool
    // Refuse an `UPDATE` or `DELETE` with no `WHERE`.  False by
    // default, because those statements are real; a caller that runs
    // user-driven queries sets it and gets a fault instead of an empty
    // table.
    require_where: Bool
    // Refuse a statement containing a `QbRaw`.  The switch a build
    // turns on when it wants to prove no query in the program was
    // assembled by hand.
    forbid_raw: Bool
    // Refuse nesting deeper than this.  64 by default.
    max_depth: Int
    // Refuse more than this many bound parameters.  Every driver has a
    // ceiling — PostgreSQL's wire protocol has 65 535 — and a query
    // built from an unbounded list reaches it as a server error rather
    // than as a fault here.  Default 65 535.
    max_params: Int

// What a render produced.
pub struct QbRender
    // The buffer, with the statement appended.
    text: [u8]
    // The values, in the order their placeholders appear.  Hand this
    // to the driver exactly as it is; the order is the render's and
    // reordering it is how a query binds the tenant id to the row
    // limit.
    params: [qbast.QbValue]

// ── The walk, and the bytes it makes ─────────────────────────────────
//
// ONE `Emit` BETWEEN TWENTY FUNCTIONS.  The buffer, the parameter
// list, the faults, the next placeholder, the depth and the options
// travel in one struct that every function in this section is handed
// as its first parameter, so each one only has to say what to write
// and where in the statement it is.  The order of the calls below is
// the order of the bytes, and the order of the bytes is the order of
// the placeholders — which is the invariant the whole package rests
// on, and the reason there is no second traversal to drift from this
// one.
//
// A FAULT IS RECORDED TWICE OVER.  Every fault goes into `faults`, in
// the order the walk met it, which is what `check` answers; the ones a
// render refuses on also go into `stops`, and `render` answers the
// first of those.  The two lists differ for the dialect limits that
// have a correct spelling anyway — `TRUE` is written `1` in SQLite and
// reported, not refused — so the distinction lives in `refuses` alone.

// A path into the statement, in the AST's spelling: `joins[0].on`,
// `columns[2].expr`.  Its own function rather than an inline join,
// because a `+` written inside a match arm strands a cell whether or
// not the arm runs — `language/a-join-in-a-match-arm-is-leaked-even-
// when-the-arm-is-not-taken`, fixed in 0.20.3, still live in the
// 0.20.2 toolchain this package is verified against — and every path
// here is built inside an arm.
fn indexed(base: Str, i: Int) -> Str
    base + "[" + str.from_int(i) + "]"

// A field of the thing at `base`: `joins[0].on`, or `on` at the top
// level, because the statement has no name.
fn field(base: Str, name: Str) -> Str
    if base == ""
        name
    else
        base + "." + name

// What the walk carries: the bytes so far, the values so far, what it
// has refused, what it has only noticed, and where it is.
struct Emit
    // The bytes, when writing.
    var bytes: [u8]
    // How many bytes there are, whether writing them or only counting.
    var count: Int
    // The values, in placeholder order.
    var params: [qbast.QbValue]
    // Every fault, in the order the walk met it.  What `check` answers.
    var faults: [qberror.QbFault]
    // The faults a render refuses on.  What `render` answers the first
    // of.
    var stops: [qberror.QbFault]
    // The position the next placeholder gets, counted from zero.
    var next_param: Int
    // How many subqueries deep the walk is.
    var depth: Int
    // Count bytes and append none.  `render_len` sets it.
    counting: Bool
    // What the caller allowed.
    o: QbRenderOptions

// The state a walk starts in.  `first_param` is where the numbering
// starts, which is what `render_expr` is for.
fn emitter(o: QbRenderOptions, counting: Bool, first_param: Int) -> Emit
    Emit { bytes: [], count: 0, params: [], faults: [], stops: [],
           next_param: first_param, depth: 0, counting: counting, o: o }

// The options for a dialect with nothing changed: the defaults every
// published call starts from.
fn plain_options(d: qbdialect.QbDialect) -> QbRenderOptions
    QbRenderOptions { dialect: d, pretty: false, quote_all: false,
                      require_where: false, forbid_raw: false,
                      max_depth: 64, max_params: 65535 }

// The zero byte a rendered statement ends with.  One function so that
// `render` appends it and `render_len` counts it by the same
// definition, and `render_str` strips exactly what they agree on.
fn terminator() -> [u8]
    [0 as u8]

// One byte: counted either way, appended when writing.
fn emit_byte(var st: Emit, b: Int)
    st.count = st.count + 1
    if not st.counting
        list.push(st.bytes, b as u8)

// A whole `Str`, byte by byte.  Not `bytes.from_str`: the counting
// pass would then have to build the buffer it is counting.
fn emit_text(var st: Emit, s: Str)
    var i = 0
    while i < str.len(s)
        emit_byte(var st, str.byte_at_or(s, i, 0))
        i = i + 1

// An `Int` written as digits.
fn emit_int(var st: Emit, n: Int)
    emit_text(var st, str.from_int(n))

// The space between two clauses: one space in the flat form, a newline
// and an indent in the pretty one.  Every clause boundary goes through
// here, and nowhere else, so the two forms cannot disagree about
// anything but the whitespace.
fn gap(var st: Emit)
    if st.o.pretty
        emit_byte(var st, 10)
        emit_byte(var st, 32)
        emit_byte(var st, 32)
    else
        emit_byte(var st, 32)

// A fault, into both lists: always into `faults`, and into `stops`
// when a render refuses on it.
fn record(var st: Emit, kind: qberror.QbFaultKind, at: Str)
    let f = qberror.fault(kind, at)
    list.push(st.faults, f)
    if refuses(kind)
        list.push(st.stops, f)

// Whether the render refuses on this kind rather than writing past it.
// A statement that is malformed is refused everywhere; a dialect limit
// is refused when there is no spelling for it at all, and only
// reported when the dialect writes something equivalent instead.
fn refuses(k: qberror.QbFaultKind) -> Bool
    match k
        QbUnsupported(feature, dialect)     => feature_refuses(feature)
        QbUnquotableIdent(name, dialect)    => true
        QbUnwritableIdent(name)             => true
        QbSubqueryNeedsAlias                => true
        QbJoinWithoutOn                     => true
        QbEmptyInList                       => true
        QbCaseArity(whens, thens)           => true
        QbInsertArity(columns, values, row) => true
        QbInsertNoColumns                   => true
        QbInsertTwoSources                  => true
        QbInsertNoSource                    => true
        QbUpdateNothingSet                  => true
        QbCteName(name)                     => true
        QbSetArmOrdered                     => true
        QbTooDeep(limit)                    => true
        QbTooManyParams(limit)              => true
        QbWhereRequired                     => true
        QbRawForbidden                      => true

// Whether a dialect's not having this feature stops the render.  The
// ones it does not are the ones the dialect can write some other way:
// a boolean literal is `1`, a conflict target is dropped because MySQL
// uses whichever unique index was hit, an offset needs a `LIMIT -1`
// in front of it.  Those are still REPORTED — a port wants to see
// them — and they are not refusals.
//
// Seven of the eighteen arms cannot be reached and are marked below.
// A feature no dialect lacks is never reported at all, because `note`
// asks `qbdialect.supports` first and answers before it records; and
// the two arms that say quoting stops the render are pre-empted, since
// the one dialect that cannot quote reports a kind of its own
// (`QbUnquotableIdent`, `QbSubqueryNeedsAlias`) rather than
// `QbUnsupported`.  The arms are kept because the match is the table a
// nineteenth feature would have to be classified in, and a wildcard
// would let one through unclassified.
fn feature_refuses(f: qbdialect.QbFeature) -> Bool
    match f
        QbFeatReturning         => true
        QbFeatILike             => true
        QbFeatDistinctOn        => true
        QbFeatNullsOrdering     => true
        QbFeatFullJoin          => true
        QbFeatForUpdate         => true
        QbFeatParameters        => true
        // cov: skip — reported as QbUnquotableIdent instead
        QbFeatQuotedIdent       => true
        // cov: skip — reported as QbSubqueryNeedsAlias instead
        QbFeatAnonymousSubquery => true
        QbFeatConflictTarget    => false
        QbFeatBooleanLiteral    => false
        // cov: skip — supported by all four dialects
        QbFeatComment           => false
        // cov: skip — supported by all four dialects
        QbFeatCte               => false
        // cov: skip — supported by all four dialects
        QbFeatRecursiveCte      => false
        // cov: skip — supported by all four dialects
        QbFeatUpsert            => false
        // cov: skip — supported by all four dialects
        QbFeatRightJoin         => false
        QbFeatIsDistinctFrom    => false
        QbFeatBareOffset        => false

// Note a feature the statement uses, if this dialect has not got it.
// Called at the point the feature is written, so the fault's path is
// the clause a caller has to change.
fn note(var st: Emit, f: qbdialect.QbFeature, at: Str)
    if not qbdialect.supports(st.o.dialect, f)
        record(var st, QbUnsupported(f, st.o.dialect), at)

// One level deeper, and the ceiling checked on the way in.  Recorded
// once, at the level that broke it, rather than once per level below.
fn enter(var st: Emit)
    st.depth = st.depth + 1
    if st.depth == st.o.max_depth + 1
        record(var st, QbTooDeep(st.o.max_depth), "")

fn leave(var st: Emit)
    st.depth = st.depth - 1

// `bs` appended to `out`, answered.  `list.concat` rather than
// `list.push`, because the buffer arrives as a borrowed parameter and a
// writable name cannot be made from one ([E2038]); the answer is the
// list a caller keeps, which is what every signature here means by
// taking the buffer and answering it.
fn append_into(out: [u8], bs: [u8]) -> [u8]
    list.concat(out, bs)

// `bs` and the terminating zero, appended to `out`, answered.
fn append_terminated(out: [u8], bs: [u8]) -> [u8]
    let with_body = append_into(out, bs)
    append_into(with_body, terminator())

// How a render ends: the first fault it refuses on, or the buffer with
// the statement and its terminator on the end of it.  A refused render
// appends NOTHING to the caller's buffer — the bytes were built
// privately and are dropped — so a caller that ignores the `Err` does
// not find half a statement in their batch.
fn finished(st: Emit, out: [u8]) -> Result<QbRender, qberror.QbFault>
    if list.len(st.stops) > 0
        Err(list.get(st.stops, 0))
    else
        Ok(QbRender { text: append_terminated(out, st.bytes), params: st.params })

// ── Identifiers and literals ─────────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE TWO PLACES A VALUE COULD BECOME SYNTAX, and the only two.  An
// identifier is written as an identifier and a value is written as a
// value, and nothing a caller put in either is parsed as anything
// else: that is the claim this package exists to make, and it is made
// by these two functions rather than by the forty that call them.

// Whether `name` is reserved in any of the four dialects.  A name this
// package would quote for one of them is a name whose meaning depends
// on the dialect, which is the fact `write_ident` needs about a
// dialect that cannot quote at all.
fn reserved_somewhere(name: Str) -> Bool
    var found = false
    for d in qbdialect.dialects()
        if qbdialect.is_reserved(d, name)
            found = true
    found

// Whether `name` has to be quoted for `d`.  A name that is not a bare
// identifier there needs quotes; and for a dialect with no quoting at
// all, so does a name another dialect reserves — there is no spelling
// of `user` that survives the trip, so the answer is a fault rather
// than a statement that works on one engine and not the next.
fn needs_quotes(d: qbdialect.QbDialect, name: Str) -> Bool
    if qbdialect.is_bare_ident(d, name)
        qbdialect.quote_char(d) == 0 and reserved_somewhere(name)
    else
        true

// Whether `name` can be written at all: non-empty and holding neither
// the dialect's own quote character nor a zero byte.  A quote inside
// quotes would need doubling, which this package refuses to guess at
// because the dialect's escape is not the one every reader assumes,
// and a zero byte ends the string somewhere downstream.
fn writable_name(d: qbdialect.QbDialect, name: Str) -> Bool
    if str.len(name) == 0
        false
    else
        var ok = true
        var i = 0
        while i < str.len(name)
            let b = str.byte_at_or(name, i, 0)
            if b == 0 or b == qbdialect.quote_char(d)
                ok = false
            i = i + 1
        ok

// What is wrong with writing `name` for `d`, or `None` when it is
// writable.  One function so that the public `write_ident` and the
// statement walk answer the same fault for the same name, with only
// the path differing.
fn ident_kind(name: Str, d: qbdialect.QbDialect, always: Bool) -> ?qberror.QbFaultKind
    if not writable_name(d, name)
        Some(QbUnwritableIdent(name))
    else if always or needs_quotes(d, name)
        if qbdialect.quote_char(d) == 0
            Some(QbUnquotableIdent(name, d))
        else
            None
    else
        None

// An identifier's bytes.  The caller has already established that they
// are writable; this only decides whether the quotes go around them.
fn emit_ident_bytes(var st: Emit, name: Str, d: qbdialect.QbDialect, always: Bool)
    if always or needs_quotes(d, name)
        emit_byte(var st, qbdialect.quote_char(d))
        emit_text(var st, name)
        emit_byte(var st, qbdialect.quote_char(d))
    else
        emit_text(var st, name)

// One identifier into the statement, at `at`, or the fault that stops
// the render.  Every column, alias, table and CTE name in this module
// goes through here.
fn emit_ident(var st: Emit, name: Str, always: Bool, at: Str)
    match ident_kind(name, st.o.dialect, always)
        Some(k) => record(var st, k, at)
        None    => emit_ident_bytes(var st, name, st.o.dialect, always)

// A doubled single quote: the escape for a quote inside a SQL string
// in all four dialects, and the reason a value cannot end the string
// it is written in.
fn emit_quote_escape(var st: Emit)
    emit_text(var st, "''")

// A doubled backslash: what MySQL needs inside a string — with
// `NO_BACKSLASH_ESCAPES` off, which is the default and which this
// package cannot see — and what the other three must not be given,
// because for them a backslash is a byte like any other.
fn emit_backslash_escape(var st: Emit)
    emit_byte(var st, 92)
    emit_byte(var st, 92)

// A `Str` as a SQL string literal, escaped for the dialect.  Written
// byte by byte: a `Str` may hold any byte but a zero, and none of them
// is special to the writer — which is the whole claim.
fn emit_text_literal(var st: Emit, s: Str)
    emit_byte(var st, 39)
    var i = 0
    while i < str.len(s)
        let b = str.byte_at_or(s, i, 0)
        if b == 39
            emit_quote_escape(var st)
        else if b == 92 and qbdialect.backslash_escapes(st.o.dialect)
            emit_backslash_escape(var st)
        else
            emit_byte(var st, b)
        i = i + 1
    emit_byte(var st, 39)

// Bytes as the dialect's blob literal: `X'4142'` for three of them and
// `0x4142` for MySQL.  Hex, so no byte in the payload needs an escape.
fn emit_blob(var st: Emit, bs: [u8])
    emit_text(var st, qbdialect.blob_prefix(st.o.dialect))
    emit_text(var st, bytes.to_hex(bytes.from_byte_list(bs)))
    emit_text(var st, qbdialect.blob_suffix(st.o.dialect))

// One value written into the SQL text.  `QbLit` comes through here,
// and so does the public `write_literal`.
fn emit_lit_value(var st: Emit, v: qbast.QbValue, at: Str)
    match v
        QbNull     => emit_text(var st, "NULL")
        QbInt(n)   => emit_int(var st, n)
        QbFloat(x) => emit_text(var st, str.from_float(x))
        QbBool(b)  =>
            note(var st, QbFeatBooleanLiteral, at)
            emit_text(var st, qbdialect.boolean_literal(st.o.dialect, b))
        QbText(s)  => emit_text_literal(var st, s)
        QbBlob(bs) => emit_blob(var st, bs)

// One bound value: the dialect's placeholder for the position it
// landed in, then the value itself into the parameter list.  The two
// are written by these six lines and by nothing else, which is why
// they cannot get out of step.
fn emit_bind(var st: Emit, v: qbast.QbValue, at: Str)
    note(var st, QbFeatParameters, at)
    if list.len(st.params) == st.o.max_params
        record(var st, QbTooManyParams(st.o.max_params), "")
    emit_text(var st, qbdialect.placeholder(st.o.dialect, st.next_param))
    st.next_param = st.next_param + 1
    list.push(st.params, v)

// ── Expressions ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// PRECEDENCE IS HANDLED BY PARENTHESISING, NOT BY KNOWING IT.  An
// operand that is itself an operator is wrapped, so `a = b AND c = d`
// keeps its meaning wherever it is spliced in, and the wrapping rules
// are the two functions below rather than a table of levels nobody can
// check.  The two exceptions are the ones the assertions in the
// shipped suite pin: an `AND`/`OR` operand is NOT wrapped when it is a
// comparison, and a comparison operands' `AND`/`OR` IS wrapped.

// Whether `e` is an operator, and so needs brackets wherever it is an
// operand.
fn is_operator(e: qbast.QbExpr) -> Bool
    match e
        QbCmp(op, lhs, rhs)   => true
        QbBinop(op, lhs, rhs) => true
        QbAnd(lhs, rhs)       => true
        QbOr(lhs, rhs)        => true
        QbNot(operand)        => true
        _                     => false

// Whether `e` is a boolean connective — the one thing an `AND`/`OR`
// operand needs brackets for, because `a AND b AND c` groups either
// way and `a OR b AND c` does not.
fn is_connective(e: qbast.QbExpr) -> Bool
    match e
        QbAnd(lhs, rhs) => true
        QbOr(lhs, rhs)  => true
        _               => false

// An operand of a comparison or an arithmetic operator: bracketed when
// it is an operator of its own.
fn emit_operand(var st: Emit, e: qbast.QbExpr, at: Str)
    if is_operator(e)
        emit_text(var st, "(")
        emit_expr(var st, e, at)
        emit_text(var st, ")")
    else
        emit_expr(var st, e, at)

// An operand of `AND` or `OR`.
fn emit_connective_operand(var st: Emit, e: qbast.QbExpr, at: Str)
    if is_connective(e)
        emit_text(var st, "(")
        emit_expr(var st, e, at)
        emit_text(var st, ")")
    else
        emit_expr(var st, e, at)

// The comparison operator's spelling.  `QbDistinct` never reaches
// here: MySQL writes it as an inverted `<=>` and the branch that does
// is in `emit_cmp`.
fn emit_cmp_word(var st: Emit, op: qbast.QbCmpOp)
    match op
        QbEq       => emit_text(var st, "=")
        QbNe       => emit_text(var st, "<>")
        QbLt       => emit_text(var st, "<")
        QbLe       => emit_text(var st, "<=")
        QbGt       => emit_text(var st, ">")
        QbGe       => emit_text(var st, ">=")
        QbDistinct => emit_text(var st, "IS DISTINCT FROM")

// A comparison.  MySQL has no `IS DISTINCT FROM` and spells the same
// question `a <=> b` with the opposite sense, so the render writes
// `NOT (a <=> b)` and reports the translation under `QbFeatIsDistinctFrom`
// for a caller who wants to see it happen.
fn emit_cmp(var st: Emit, op: qbast.QbCmpOp, lhs: qbast.QbExpr, rhs: qbast.QbExpr, at: Str)
    if op == qbast.QbDistinct and st.o.dialect == QbMysql
        // `record`, not `note`: MySQL HAS the feature — the table says
        // so, because `<=>` asks the same question — and what a caller
        // has to see is that a translation happened, which is exactly
        // what the row is for.
        record(var st, QbUnsupported(QbFeatIsDistinctFrom, st.o.dialect), at)
        emit_text(var st, "NOT (")
        emit_operand(var st, lhs, field(at, "lhs"))
        emit_text(var st, " <=> ")
        emit_operand(var st, rhs, field(at, "rhs"))
        emit_text(var st, ")")
    else
        emit_operand(var st, lhs, field(at, "lhs"))
        emit_text(var st, " ")
        emit_cmp_word(var st, op)
        emit_text(var st, " ")
        emit_operand(var st, rhs, field(at, "rhs"))

// The arithmetic and bitwise operator's spelling, and `||` for a
// concatenation.  MySQL's `CONCAT(a, b)` is a call rather than an
// operator and lives in the branch in `emit_binop`.
fn emit_binop_word(var st: Emit, op: qbast.QbBinOp)
    match op
        QbAdd    => emit_text(var st, "+")
        QbSub    => emit_text(var st, "-")
        QbMul    => emit_text(var st, "*")
        QbDiv    => emit_text(var st, "/")
        QbMod    => emit_text(var st, "%")
        QbConcat => emit_text(var st, "||")
        QbBitAnd => emit_text(var st, "&")
        QbBitOr  => emit_text(var st, "|")
        QbShl    => emit_text(var st, "<<")
        QbShr    => emit_text(var st, ">>")

// An arithmetic, bitwise or concatenating operation.  MySQL needs
// `CONCAT(a, b)` for a `||` unless the server was started with
// `PIPES_AS_CONCAT`, which this package cannot see.
fn emit_binop(var st: Emit, op: qbast.QbBinOp, lhs: qbast.QbExpr, rhs: qbast.QbExpr, at: Str)
    if op == qbast.QbConcat and st.o.dialect == QbMysql
        emit_text(var st, "CONCAT(")
        emit_operand(var st, lhs, field(at, "lhs"))
        emit_text(var st, ", ")
        emit_operand(var st, rhs, field(at, "rhs"))
        emit_text(var st, ")")
    else
        emit_operand(var st, lhs, field(at, "lhs"))
        emit_text(var st, " ")
        emit_binop_word(var st, op)
        emit_text(var st, " ")
        emit_operand(var st, rhs, field(at, "rhs"))

// Expressions separated by commas, each at its own index: the items of
// a `SELECT`, a function's arguments, a `GROUP BY` list.
fn emit_exprs(var st: Emit, es: [qbast.QbExpr], base: Str)
    var i = 0
    for e in es
        if i > 0
            emit_text(var st, ", ")
        emit_expr(var st, e, indexed(base, i))
        i = i + 1

// `IN (...)`, `NOT IN (...)`.
fn emit_in(var st: Emit, operand: qbast.QbExpr, values: [qbast.QbExpr], negated: Bool, at: Str)
    if list.len(values) == 0
        record(var st, QbEmptyInList, at)
    emit_operand(var st, operand, field(at, "operand"))
    if negated
        emit_text(var st, " NOT IN (")
    else
        emit_text(var st, " IN (")
    emit_exprs(var st, values, field(at, "values"))
    emit_text(var st, ")")

// `CASE WHEN a THEN b … ELSE c END`.  The branches are interleaved and
// the shorter list stops the loop, so a `QbCase` whose halves disagree
// is refused and still cannot crash the render.
fn emit_case(var st: Emit, whens: [qbast.QbExpr], thens: [qbast.QbExpr], otherwise: ?qbast.QbExpr,
             at: Str)
    if list.len(whens) != list.len(thens)
        record(var st, QbCaseArity(list.len(whens), list.len(thens)), at)
    emit_text(var st, "CASE")
    var i = 0
    while i < list.len(whens) and i < list.len(thens)
        emit_text(var st, " WHEN ")
        emit_expr(var st, list.get(whens, i), indexed(field(at, "whens"), i))
        emit_text(var st, " THEN ")
        emit_expr(var st, list.get(thens, i), indexed(field(at, "thens"), i))
        i = i + 1
    match otherwise
        Some(alt) =>
            emit_text(var st, " ELSE ")
            emit_expr(var st, alt, field(at, "otherwise"))
        None      => ()
    emit_text(var st, " END")

// A subquery in brackets, one level deeper, with the depth ceiling
// checked on the way in.
fn emit_subquery(var st: Emit, inner: qbast.QbSelect, at: Str)
    emit_text(var st, "(")
    enter(var st)
    emit_select(var st, inner, at)
    leave(var st)
    emit_text(var st, ")")

// One expression.  THIS IS THE WALK, and the order of the arms below is
// not the order of the bytes of any one expression — each arm writes
// its own — but every arm writes its left operand before its right.
fn emit_expr(var st: Emit, e: qbast.QbExpr, at: Str)
    match e
        QbCol(name)                               => emit_ident(var st, name, st.o.quote_all, at)
        QbQual(table, column)                     =>
            emit_ident(var st, table, st.o.quote_all, field(at, "table"))
            emit_text(var st, ".")
            emit_ident(var st, column, st.o.quote_all, field(at, "column"))
        QbStar                                    => emit_text(var st, "*")
        QbQualStar(table)                         =>
            emit_ident(var st, table, st.o.quote_all, field(at, "table"))
            emit_text(var st, ".*")
        QbBind(value)                             => emit_bind(var st, value, at)
        QbLit(value)                              => emit_lit_value(var st, value, at)
        QbRaw(sql)                                =>
            if st.o.forbid_raw
                record(var st, QbRawForbidden, at)
            emit_text(var st, sql)
        QbCmp(op, lhs, rhs)                       => emit_cmp(var st, op, lhs, rhs, at)
        QbBinop(op, lhs, rhs)                     => emit_binop(var st, op, lhs, rhs, at)
        QbAnd(lhs, rhs)                           =>
            emit_connective_operand(var st, lhs, field(at, "lhs"))
            emit_text(var st, " AND ")
            emit_connective_operand(var st, rhs, field(at, "rhs"))
        QbOr(lhs, rhs)                            =>
            emit_connective_operand(var st, lhs, field(at, "lhs"))
            emit_text(var st, " OR ")
            emit_connective_operand(var st, rhs, field(at, "rhs"))
        QbNot(operand)                            =>
            emit_text(var st, "NOT (")
            emit_expr(var st, operand, field(at, "operand"))
            emit_text(var st, ")")
        QbIsNull(operand, negated)                =>
            emit_operand(var st, operand, field(at, "operand"))
            if negated
                emit_text(var st, " IS NOT NULL")
            else
                emit_text(var st, " IS NULL")
        QbIn(operand, values, negated)            => emit_in(var st, operand, values, negated, at)
        QbInSelect(operand, inner, negated)       =>
            emit_operand(var st, operand, field(at, "operand"))
            if negated
                emit_text(var st, " NOT IN ")
            else
                emit_text(var st, " IN ")
            emit_subquery(var st, inner, field(at, "inner"))
        QbBetween(operand, low, high, negated)    =>
            emit_operand(var st, operand, field(at, "operand"))
            if negated
                emit_text(var st, " NOT BETWEEN ")
            else
                emit_text(var st, " BETWEEN ")
            emit_operand(var st, low, field(at, "low"))
            emit_text(var st, " AND ")
            emit_operand(var st, high, field(at, "high"))
        QbLike(operand, pattern, escape, negated) =>
            emit_operand(var st, operand, field(at, "operand"))
            if negated
                emit_text(var st, " NOT LIKE ")
            else
                emit_text(var st, " LIKE ")
            emit_operand(var st, pattern, field(at, "pattern"))
            if escape != ""
                emit_text(var st, " ESCAPE ")
                emit_text_literal(var st, escape)
        QbILike(operand, pattern, negated)        =>
            note(var st, QbFeatILike, at)
            emit_operand(var st, operand, field(at, "operand"))
            if negated
                emit_text(var st, " NOT ILIKE ")
            else
                emit_text(var st, " ILIKE ")
            emit_operand(var st, pattern, field(at, "pattern"))
        QbFn(name, args)                          =>
            emit_text(var st, name)
            emit_text(var st, "(")
            emit_exprs(var st, args, field(at, "args"))
            emit_text(var st, ")")
        QbAgg(name, args, distinct, filter)       =>
            emit_text(var st, name)
            emit_text(var st, "(")
            if distinct
                emit_text(var st, "DISTINCT ")
            emit_exprs(var st, args, field(at, "args"))
            emit_text(var st, ")")
            match filter
                Some(cond) =>
                    emit_text(var st, " FILTER (WHERE ")
                    emit_expr(var st, cond, field(at, "filter"))
                    emit_text(var st, ")")
                None       => ()
        QbCast(operand, ty)                       =>
            emit_text(var st, "CAST(")
            emit_expr(var st, operand, field(at, "operand"))
            emit_text(var st, " AS ")
            emit_text(var st, ty)
            emit_text(var st, ")")
        QbCase(whens, thens, otherwise)           => emit_case(var st, whens, thens, otherwise, at)
        QbCoalesce(args)                          =>
            emit_text(var st, "COALESCE(")
            emit_exprs(var st, args, field(at, "args"))
            emit_text(var st, ")")
        QbExists(inner, negated)                  =>
            if negated
                emit_text(var st, "NOT EXISTS ")
            else
                emit_text(var st, "EXISTS ")
            emit_subquery(var st, inner, field(at, "inner"))
        QbScalar(inner)                           => emit_subquery(var st, inner, field(at, "inner"))

// ── The clauses ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// `ORDER BY a DESC NULLS LAST, …`.
fn emit_order(var st: Emit, terms: [qbast.QbOrder], base: Str)
    var i = 0
    for t in terms
        if i > 0
            emit_text(var st, ", ")
        let at = indexed(base, i)
        emit_expr(var st, t.expr, field(at, "expr"))
        if t.descending
            emit_text(var st, " DESC")
        if t.nulls != qbast.QbNullsDefault
            note(var st, QbFeatNullsOrdering, at)
            if t.nulls == qbast.QbNullsFirst
                emit_text(var st, " NULLS FIRST")
            else
                emit_text(var st, " NULLS LAST")
        i = i + 1

// Column names separated by commas: an `INSERT`'s column list, a
// conflict target, a CTE's column list.
fn emit_idents(var st: Emit, names: [Str], base: Str)
    var i = 0
    for n in names
        if i > 0
            emit_text(var st, ", ")
        emit_ident(var st, n, st.o.quote_all, indexed(base, i))
        i = i + 1

// `a, b AS c` — the items of a `SELECT` or a `RETURNING`.  `base` is
// spelled by the caller because the same shape is `columns` in one
// statement and `returning` in another, and a fault has to name the
// field the caller's own tree has.
fn emit_items(var st: Emit, cols: [qbast.QbColumn], base: Str, key: Str)
    if list.len(cols) == 0
        emit_text(var st, "*")
    else
        var i = 0
        for c in cols
            if i > 0
                emit_text(var st, ", ")
            let at = indexed(field(base, key), i)
            emit_expr(var st, c.expr, field(at, "expr"))
            if c.as_name != ""
                emit_text(var st, " AS ")
                emit_ident(var st, c.as_name, st.o.quote_all, field(at, "as_name"))
            i = i + 1

// `SET a = …, b = …`, which is the same shape in an `UPDATE` and in a
// `DO UPDATE`.
fn emit_assigns(var st: Emit, assigns: [qbast.QbAssign], base: Str)
    var i = 0
    for a in assigns
        if i > 0
            emit_text(var st, ", ")
        let at = indexed(base, i)
        emit_ident(var st, a.column, st.o.quote_all, field(at, "column"))
        emit_text(var st, " = ")
        emit_expr(var st, a.value, field(at, "value"))
        i = i + 1

// A table, a subquery or a CTE, with its alias.  A subquery with no
// alias is a fault in PostgreSQL and MySQL and legal in SQLite, which
// is a fact the table knows and this function asks for.
fn emit_source(var st: Emit, src: qbast.QbSource, base: Str)
    match src
        QbTable(name, as_name)     =>
            emit_ident(var st, name, st.o.quote_all, field(base, "name"))
            emit_alias(var st, as_name, base)
        QbSubquery(inner, as_name) =>
            if as_name == ""
                if not qbdialect.supports(st.o.dialect, QbFeatAnonymousSubquery)
                    record(var st, QbSubqueryNeedsAlias, base)
            emit_subquery(var st, inner, field(base, "inner"))
            emit_alias(var st, as_name, base)
        QbCteRef(name, as_name)    =>
            emit_ident(var st, name, st.o.quote_all, field(base, "name"))
            emit_alias(var st, as_name, base)

// `AS alias`, when there is one.
fn emit_alias(var st: Emit, as_name: Str, base: Str)
    if as_name != ""
        emit_text(var st, " AS ")
        emit_ident(var st, as_name, st.o.quote_all, field(base, "as_name"))

// `INNER JOIN t ON …`.  A `CROSS JOIN` takes no `ON` and a condition
// the caller wrote on one is dropped rather than written, because
// `CROSS JOIN t ON …` is not SQL any dialect accepts; every other kind
// without one is refused by name.
fn emit_join(var st: Emit, j: qbast.QbJoin, base: Str)
    emit_text(var st, " ")
    emit_join_kind(var st, j.kind, base)
    emit_text(var st, " ")
    emit_source(var st, j.source, field(base, "source"))
    match j.on
        Some(cond) =>
            if j.kind != qbast.QbCross
                emit_text(var st, " ON ")
                emit_expr(var st, cond, field(base, "on"))
        None       =>
            if j.kind != qbast.QbCross
                record(var st, QbJoinWithoutOn, field(base, "on"))

// The join's keyword.  A full join is not in MySQL and is named rather
// than written as an inner join; a right join is in all four and the
// SQLite row says so on the strength of 3.39, which this package
// cannot check.
fn emit_join_kind(var st: Emit, kind: qbast.QbJoinKind, base: Str)
    match kind
        QbInner => emit_text(var st, "INNER JOIN")
        QbLeft  => emit_text(var st, "LEFT JOIN")
        QbRight => emit_text(var st, "RIGHT JOIN")
        QbFull  =>
            note(var st, QbFeatFullJoin, base)
            emit_text(var st, "FULL OUTER JOIN")
        QbCross => emit_text(var st, "CROSS JOIN")

// `UNION ALL <select>`.  An arm's own `ORDER BY` or `LIMIT` is refused
// everywhere: SQLite allows it and PostgreSQL does not, and the
// brackets that would make it portable change what the clause applies
// to.
fn emit_arm(var st: Emit, arm: qbast.QbSetArm, base: Str)
    if list.len(arm.body.order_by) > 0 or arm.body.limit >= 0 or arm.body.offset >= 0
        record(var st, QbSetArmOrdered, field(base, "body"))
    emit_text(var st, " ")
    emit_set_word(var st, arm.op)
    emit_text(var st, " ")
    emit_select(var st, arm.body, field(base, "body"))

// The set operation's keyword.
fn emit_set_word(var st: Emit, op: qbast.QbSetOp)
    match op
        QbUnion     => emit_text(var st, "UNION")
        QbUnionAll  => emit_text(var st, "UNION ALL")
        QbIntersect => emit_text(var st, "INTERSECT")
        QbExcept    => emit_text(var st, "EXCEPT")

// `WITH RECURSIVE a AS (…), b AS (…)`.  One recursive CTE makes the
// whole keyword recursive, which is why `recursive` is read across the
// list rather than per CTE.
fn emit_with(var st: Emit, ctes: [qbast.QbCte], base: Str)
    if list.len(ctes) > 0
        note(var st, QbFeatCte, base)
        emit_text(var st, "WITH ")
        if any_recursive(ctes)
            note(var st, QbFeatRecursiveCte, base)
            emit_text(var st, "RECURSIVE ")
        var i = 0
        for c in ctes
            if i > 0
                emit_text(var st, ", ")
            let at = indexed(field(base, "ctes"), i)
            if c.name == "" or cte_name_seen(ctes, c.name, i)
                record(var st, QbCteName(c.name), at)
            emit_ident(var st, c.name, st.o.quote_all, at)
            if list.len(c.columns) > 0
                emit_text(var st, " (")
                emit_idents(var st, c.columns, field(at, "columns"))
                emit_text(var st, ")")
            emit_text(var st, " AS ")
            emit_subquery(var st, c.body, field(at, "body"))
            i = i + 1
        // The clause after the list is the statement proper, so the
        // list has to end with the gap every other clause ends with;
        // without it the first word of the statement is glued to the
        // last `)` and the whole thing is one identifier.
        gap(var st)

// Whether an earlier CTE in the list already took this name.
fn cte_name_seen(ctes: [qbast.QbCte], name: Str, i: Int) -> Bool
    var seen = false
    for j in 0..i
        let other = list.get(ctes, j)
        if other.name == name
            seen = true
    seen

// Whether any CTE in the list is recursive.
fn any_recursive(ctes: [qbast.QbCte]) -> Bool
    var found = false
    for c in ctes
        if c.recursive
            found = true
    found

// `LIMIT n OFFSET m`.  SQLite needs a `LIMIT` in front of an `OFFSET`
// and `-1` is its "no limit", so a bare offset is written as
// `LIMIT -1 OFFSET m` there rather than refused — the dialect's own
// spelling, not a translation of the caller's meaning.
fn emit_tail(var st: Emit, sel: qbast.QbSelect, base: Str)
    if sel.limit >= 0
        gap(var st)
        emit_text(var st, "LIMIT ")
        emit_int(var st, sel.limit)
    if sel.offset >= 0
        if sel.limit < 0 and not qbdialect.supports(st.o.dialect, QbFeatBareOffset)
            // A translation rather than a refusal, and reported for the
            // same reason as the other three: the statement the caller
            // wrote is not the statement the server will read.
            note(var st, QbFeatBareOffset, field(base, "offset"))
            gap(var st)
            emit_text(var st, "LIMIT -1")
        gap(var st)
        emit_text(var st, "OFFSET ")
        emit_int(var st, sel.offset)

// The rows of an `INSERT … VALUES`, one bracket per row, with each
// row's length checked against the column list before it is written.
fn emit_rows(var st: Emit, ins: qbast.QbInsert, base: Str)
    var r = 0
    for row in ins.rows
        if r > 0
            emit_text(var st, ", ")
        let at = indexed(field(base, "rows"), r)
        if list.len(row) != list.len(ins.columns)
            record(var st, QbInsertArity(list.len(ins.columns), list.len(row), r), at)
        emit_text(var st, "(")
        var c = 0
        for cell in row
            if c > 0
                emit_text(var st, ", ")
            emit_expr(var st, cell, indexed(at, c))
            c = c + 1
        emit_text(var st, ")")
        r = r + 1

// `ON CONFLICT …`, or MySQL's spelling of the same two ideas.  MySQL
// has no conflict target at all — it uses whichever unique index was
// hit — so a statement that named one is reported and written without
// it rather than refused, because the upsert still does what the
// caller asked for on the index the server picks.
fn emit_conflict(var st: Emit, ins: qbast.QbInsert, base: Str)
    if ins.conflict != qbast.QbConflictError
        if st.o.dialect == QbMysql
            if list.len(ins.conflict_columns) > 0
                // MySQL renders the upsert and drops the target, so
                // the drop is REPORTED rather than silent — the feature
                // row exists precisely so a caller can see it happen.
                note(var st, QbFeatConflictTarget, field(base, "conflict_columns"))
            if ins.conflict == qbast.QbConflictUpdate
                emit_text(var st, " ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE ")
                emit_assigns(var st, ins.conflict_set, field(base, "conflict_set"))
        else
            emit_text(var st, " ON CONFLICT ")
            if list.len(ins.conflict_columns) > 0
                note(var st, QbFeatConflictTarget, field(base, "conflict_columns"))
                emit_text(var st, "(")
                emit_idents(var st, ins.conflict_columns,
                            field(base, "conflict_columns"))
                emit_text(var st, ") ")
            if ins.conflict == qbast.QbConflictIgnore
                emit_text(var st, "DO NOTHING")
            else
                emit_text(var st, "DO UPDATE SET ")
                emit_assigns(var st, ins.conflict_set, field(base, "conflict_set"))
                match ins.conflict_where
                    Some(cond) =>
                        emit_text(var st, " WHERE ")
                        emit_expr(var st, cond, field(base, "conflict_where"))
                    None       => ()

// `RETURNING a, b`, or the dialect limit when the dialect has none.
fn emit_returning(var st: Emit, cols: [qbast.QbColumn], base: Str)
    if list.len(cols) > 0
        note(var st, QbFeatReturning, base)
        gap(var st)
        emit_text(var st, "RETURNING ")
        emit_items(var st, cols, base, "returning")

// ── The statements ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

// A `SELECT`, clause by clause in the order SQL writes them.  The
// `WITH` list first, then the arms of a set operation, then the
// parent's own `ORDER BY` and `LIMIT` — which is what those clauses
// apply to, and the reason `qbast`'s walk is ordered the same way.
fn emit_select(var st: Emit, sel: qbast.QbSelect, base: Str)
    emit_with(var st, sel.ctes, base)
    emit_text(var st, "SELECT ")
    // `DISTINCT ON (…)` is one clause, not a `DISTINCT` with an `ON`
    // after it: PostgreSQL parses the three words together, and
    // `SELECT ON (x) *` is a syntax error.  So `distinct_on` writes
    // its own `DISTINCT` and a select that carried both gets one.
    if list.len(sel.distinct_on) > 0
        note(var st, QbFeatDistinctOn, field(base, "distinct_on"))
        emit_text(var st, "DISTINCT ON (")
        emit_exprs(var st, sel.distinct_on, field(base, "distinct_on"))
        emit_text(var st, ") ")
    else if sel.distinct
        emit_text(var st, "DISTINCT ")
    emit_items(var st, sel.columns, base, "columns")
    match sel.from
        Some(src) =>
            gap(var st)
            emit_text(var st, "FROM ")
            emit_source(var st, src, field(base, "from"))
        None      => ()
    var i = 0
    for j in sel.joins
        emit_join(var st, j, indexed(field(base, "joins"), i))
        i = i + 1
    match sel.where_
        Some(cond) =>
            gap(var st)
            emit_text(var st, "WHERE ")
            emit_expr(var st, cond, field(base, "where_"))
        None       => ()
    if list.len(sel.group_by) > 0
        gap(var st)
        emit_text(var st, "GROUP BY ")
        emit_exprs(var st, sel.group_by, field(base, "group_by"))
    match sel.having
        Some(cond) =>
            gap(var st)
            emit_text(var st, "HAVING ")
            emit_expr(var st, cond, field(base, "having"))
        None       => ()
    i = 0
    for arm in sel.set_arms
        emit_arm(var st, arm, indexed(field(base, "set_arms"), i))
        i = i + 1
    if list.len(sel.order_by) > 0
        gap(var st)
        emit_text(var st, "ORDER BY ")
        emit_order(var st, sel.order_by, field(base, "order_by"))
    emit_tail(var st, sel, base)
    if sel.for_update
        note(var st, QbFeatForUpdate, base)
        gap(var st)
        emit_text(var st, "FOR UPDATE")

// `WITH …, INSERT INTO t (…) VALUES …|SELECT …, ON CONFLICT …,
// RETURNING …`.  MySQL's `INSERT IGNORE` goes at the keyword rather
// than at the conflict clause, which is why the conflict is read
// before the target is written.
fn emit_insert(var st: Emit, ins: qbast.QbInsert, base: Str)
    emit_with(var st, ins.ctes, base)
    emit_text(var st, "INSERT ")
    if ins.conflict == qbast.QbConflictIgnore and st.o.dialect == QbMysql
        emit_text(var st, "IGNORE ")
    emit_text(var st, "INTO ")
    emit_ident(var st, ins.table, st.o.quote_all, field(base, "table"))
    if list.len(ins.columns) == 0
        record(var st, QbInsertNoColumns, base)
    else
        emit_text(var st, " (")
        emit_idents(var st, ins.columns, field(base, "columns"))
        emit_text(var st, ")")
    match ins.from_select
        Some(inner) =>
            if list.len(ins.rows) > 0
                record(var st, QbInsertTwoSources, base)
            gap(var st)
            emit_subquery(var st, inner, field(base, "from_select"))
        None        =>
            if list.len(ins.rows) == 0
                record(var st, QbInsertNoSource, base)
            else
                gap(var st)
                emit_text(var st, "VALUES ")
                emit_rows(var st, ins, base)
    emit_conflict(var st, ins, base)
    emit_returning(var st, ins.returning, base)

// `WITH …, UPDATE t SET … FROM … WHERE … RETURNING …`.  An `UPDATE`
// with nothing set is refused; one with no `WHERE` is not, unless the
// caller turned `require_where` on.
fn emit_update(var st: Emit, upd: qbast.QbUpdate, base: Str)
    emit_with(var st, upd.ctes, base)
    emit_text(var st, "UPDATE ")
    emit_ident(var st, upd.table, st.o.quote_all, field(base, "table"))
    if list.len(upd.set) == 0
        record(var st, QbUpdateNothingSet, base)
    else
        gap(var st)
        emit_text(var st, "SET ")
        emit_assigns(var st, upd.set, field(base, "set"))
    match upd.from
        Some(src) =>
            gap(var st)
            emit_text(var st, "FROM ")
            emit_source(var st, src, field(base, "from"))
        None      => ()
    match upd.where_
        Some(cond) =>
            gap(var st)
            emit_text(var st, "WHERE ")
            emit_expr(var st, cond, field(base, "where_"))
        None       =>
            if st.o.require_where
                record(var st, QbWhereRequired, "")
    emit_returning(var st, upd.returning, base)

// `WITH …, DELETE FROM t WHERE … RETURNING …`.
fn emit_delete(var st: Emit, del: qbast.QbDelete, base: Str)
    emit_with(var st, del.ctes, base)
    emit_text(var st, "DELETE FROM ")
    emit_ident(var st, del.table, st.o.quote_all, field(base, "table"))
    match del.where_
        Some(cond) =>
            gap(var st)
            emit_text(var st, "WHERE ")
            emit_expr(var st, cond, field(base, "where_"))
        None       =>
            if st.o.require_where
                record(var st, QbWhereRequired, "")
    emit_returning(var st, del.returning, base)

fn emit_stmt(var st: Emit, s: qbast.QbStmt)
    match s
        QbStmtSelect(body) => emit_select(var st, body, "")
        QbStmtInsert(body) => emit_insert(var st, body, "")
        QbStmtUpdate(body) => emit_update(var st, body, "")
        QbStmtDelete(body) => emit_delete(var st, body, "")

// The defaults for a dialect: not pretty, quote only what needs it,
// allow an unguarded write, allow raw, depth 64, 65 535 parameters.
//
// ```novo
// use qbrender
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${qbrender.options(QbPostgres).max_depth}")
// ```
pub fn options(d: qbdialect.QbDialect) -> QbRenderOptions []
    plain_options(d)

// `o` with pretty-printing on.
//
// ```novo
// use qbrender
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${qbrender.with_pretty(qbrender.options(QbSqlite), true).pretty}")
// ```
pub fn with_pretty(o: QbRenderOptions, pretty: Bool) -> QbRenderOptions []
    QbRenderOptions { dialect: o.dialect, pretty: pretty, quote_all: o.quote_all,
                      require_where: o.require_where, forbid_raw: o.forbid_raw,
                      max_depth: o.max_depth, max_params: o.max_params }

// `o` refusing an `UPDATE` or `DELETE` with no `WHERE`.
//
// ```novo
// use qbrender
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let o = qbrender.with_require_where(qbrender.options(QbSqlite), true)
//     println("${o.require_where}")
// ```
pub fn with_require_where(o: QbRenderOptions, require: Bool) -> QbRenderOptions []
    QbRenderOptions { dialect: o.dialect, pretty: o.pretty, quote_all: o.quote_all,
                      require_where: require, forbid_raw: o.forbid_raw,
                      max_depth: o.max_depth, max_params: o.max_params }

// `o` refusing a statement that contains a `QbRaw`.
//
// ```novo
// use qbrender
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let o = qbrender.with_forbid_raw(qbrender.options(QbSqlite), true)
//     println("${o.forbid_raw}")
// ```
pub fn with_forbid_raw(o: QbRenderOptions, forbid: Bool) -> QbRenderOptions []
    QbRenderOptions { dialect: o.dialect, pretty: o.pretty, quote_all: o.quote_all,
                      require_where: o.require_where, forbid_raw: forbid,
                      max_depth: o.max_depth, max_params: o.max_params }

// A statement appended to `out`, with its parameters.
//
// The one call the package exists for.  Stops at the first fault,
// which is what a caller about to execute wants; `check` answers all
// of them.  A refused render appends nothing to `out`.
//
// ```novo
// use std.list
// use qbast
// use qbbuild
// use qbrender
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let s = qbbuild.from(qbbuild.select(), "users", "")
//     match qbrender.render([], QbStmtSelect(s), qbrender.options(QbSqlite))
//         Ok(r)  => println("${list.len(r.text)}")
//         Err(_) => println("no")
// ```
pub fn render(out: [u8], s: qbast.QbStmt,
              o: QbRenderOptions) -> Result<QbRender, qberror.QbFault> []
    var st = emitter(o, false, 0)
    emit_stmt(var st, s)
    finished(st, out)

// A statement as a string, with its parameters — the convenience over
// `render` for a caller that is not batching.  The terminating zero
// `render` appends is stripped: `sql` is the statement.
//
// ```novo
// use qbast
// use qbbuild
// use qbrender
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let s = qbbuild.from(qbbuild.select(), "users", "")
//     match qbrender.render_str(QbStmtSelect(s), qbrender.options(QbSqlite))
//         Ok(r)  => println("${r.params}")
//         Err(_) => println("no")
// ```
pub fn render_str(s: qbast.QbStmt, o: QbRenderOptions) -> Result<QbRenderedText, qberror.QbFault> []
    match render([], s, o)
        Err(f) => Err(f)
        Ok(r)  => rendered_text(r)

// The `Str` form of a rendered statement.
fn rendered_text(r: QbRender) -> Result<QbRenderedText, qberror.QbFault>
    let n = list.len(r.text) - list.len(terminator())
    let body = bytes.byte_list_to_str(list.slice(r.text, 0, n))
    Ok(QbRenderedText { sql: body, params: r.params })

// A rendered statement as text.
pub struct QbRenderedText
    // The SQL.
    sql: Str
    // The values, in placeholder order.
    params: [qbast.QbValue]

// One expression appended to `out`, with its parameters numbered from
// `first_param`.
//
// The door for a caller splicing a condition into SQL it wrote itself
// — a migration, a view body — who needs the numbering to continue
// from where their own placeholders left off.  Getting that number
// wrong is the failure this signature exists to make visible.
//
// ```novo
// use std.list
// use qbbuild
// use qbrender
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let e = qbbuild.eq("id", qbbuild.int_value(7))
//     match qbrender.render_expr([], e, qbrender.options(QbPostgres), 0)
//         Ok(r)  => println("${list.len(r.text)}")
//         Err(_) => println("no")
// ```
pub fn render_expr(out: [u8], e: qbast.QbExpr, o: QbRenderOptions,
                   first_param: Int) -> Result<QbRender, qberror.QbFault> []
    var st = emitter(o, false, first_param)
    emit_expr(var st, e, "")
    finished(st, out)

// Every fault in `s` for `o`'s dialect, in the order the render would
// have met them.
//
// An empty list means `render` will succeed.  What a test asserts and
// what a tool porting a program to another dialect reads.  The dialect
// limits that have a correct spelling anyway (`TRUE` in SQLite, a
// conflict target in MySQL) appear here and would not stop a render.
//
// ```novo
// use std.list
// use qbast
// use qbbuild
// use qbrender
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let s = qbbuild.select()
//     println("${list.len(qbrender.check(QbStmtSelect(s), qbrender.options(QbSqlite)))}")
// ```
pub fn check(s: qbast.QbStmt, o: QbRenderOptions) -> [qberror.QbFault] []
    var st = emitter(o, true, 0)
    emit_stmt(var st, s)
    st.faults

// The features `s` uses that `d` does not have, one fault each.
//
// The port question on its own: a caller moving from SQLite to
// PostgreSQL calls this for every statement in their program and gets
// the list of things to rewrite, without the malformed-statement
// faults mixed in.  A dialect limit that the render writes another way
// is in this list too, which is what a port wants to see.
//
// ```novo
// use std.list
// use qbast
// use qbbuild
// use qbrender
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let s = qbbuild.select()
//     println("${list.len(qbrender.unsupported(QbStmtSelect(s), QbMysql))}")
// ```
pub fn unsupported(s: qbast.QbStmt, d: qbdialect.QbDialect) -> [qberror.QbFault] []
    qberror.dialect_limits(check(s, plain_options(d)))

// True when `s` is an `UPDATE` or `DELETE` with no `WHERE` — a
// statement that touches every row.
//
// Not refused by default, because "set every row's flag" is a real
// statement; published as a question so a review, a test or a
// production guard can ask it.
//
// ```novo
// use qbast
// use qbbuild
// use qbrender
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${qbrender.unguarded(QbStmtDelete(qbbuild.delete("sessions")))}")
// ```
pub fn unguarded(s: qbast.QbStmt) -> Bool []
    match s
        QbStmtUpdate(body) => absent(body.where_)
        QbStmtDelete(body) => absent(body.where_)
        QbStmtSelect(body) => false
        QbStmtInsert(body) => false

// Whether an optional condition is absent.  Its own function because
// the two arms above are the question this call answers, and a `match`
// inside a `match` arm is a shape the 0.20.2 compiler strands cells in
// (memory-perceus/a-nested-match-with-a-method-answer-arm-leaks-the-
// enclosing-subject).
fn absent(cond: ?qbast.QbExpr) -> Bool
    match cond
        Some(_) => false
        None    => true

// How many bytes `render` will append, without appending them.
//
// Exact, not an estimate: the same walk, counting instead of writing,
// and counting the terminating zero `render` appends.
//
// ```novo
// use qbast
// use qbbuild
// use qbrender
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let s = qbbuild.from(qbbuild.select(), "users", "")
//     match qbrender.render_len(QbStmtSelect(s), qbrender.options(QbSqlite))
//         Ok(n)  => println("${n}")
//         Err(_) => println("no")
// ```
pub fn render_len(s: qbast.QbStmt, o: QbRenderOptions) -> Result<Int, qberror.QbFault> []
    var st = emitter(o, true, 0)
    emit_stmt(var st, s)
    if list.len(st.stops) > 0
        Err(list.get(st.stops, 0))
    else
        Ok(st.count + list.len(terminator()))

// One identifier appended to `out`, quoted if it needs quoting in this
// dialect.
//
// Published because it is the piece of this package a caller writing
// their own SQL most often needs, and the piece they are most likely
// to get wrong — the escape for a quote inside a quoted identifier is
// to double it, not to backslash it, in all three dialects that can
// quote at all.
//
// `QbUnquotableIdent` for a name that needs quoting in a dialect that
// cannot quote: a name that is not a bare identifier there, or one
// another dialect reserves, in `QbSqlEngine`.  No zero byte is
// appended — this is called in the middle of a statement — which is
// why its answer is one byte shorter than `write_literal`'s for the
// same name.
//
// ```novo
// use std.list
// use qbrender
//
// fn main() [io]
//     match qbrender.write_ident([], "user", QbPostgres, false)
//         Ok(b)  => println("${list.len(b)}")
//         Err(_) => println("no")
// ```
pub fn write_ident(out: [u8], name: Str, d: qbdialect.QbDialect,
                   always: Bool) -> Result<[u8], qberror.QbFault> []
    match ident_kind(name, d, always)
        Some(k) => Err(qberror.fault(k, ""))
        None    =>
            var st = emitter(plain_options(d), false, 0)
            emit_ident_bytes(var st, name, d, always)
            Ok(append_into(out, st.bytes))

// One value appended to `out` as a LITERAL, escaped for this dialect.
//
// For `QbLit`, and for a caller writing their own SQL around a
// constant they control.  NOT a substitute for binding: an escaped
// literal is still a value in the query's text, which is what changes
// the plan cache and what makes a log of the SQL a log of the data.
//
// The answer ends with the same zero byte `render` appends.
//
// ```novo
// use std.list
// use qbbuild
// use qbrender
//
// fn main() [io]
//     match qbrender.write_literal([], qbbuild.text_value("o'brien"), QbSqlite)
//         Ok(b)  => println("${list.len(b)}")
//         Err(_) => println("no")
// ```
pub fn write_literal(out: [u8], v: qbast.QbValue,
                     d: qbdialect.QbDialect) -> Result<[u8], qberror.QbFault> []
    var st = emitter(plain_options(d), false, 0)
    emit_lit_value(var st, v, "")
    if list.len(st.stops) > 0
        // cov: skip — one value literal records no refusal: the only
        // feature writing one can use, the boolean literal, is
        // reported and not refused, so this render never fills `stops`
        Err(list.get(st.stops, 0))
    else
        Ok(append_terminated(out, st.bytes))

// How many `QbRaw` expressions `s` contains — `qbast.raw_count`, named
// here too because this is the module a reviewer is reading when they
// want to know.
//
// ```novo
// use qbast
// use qbbuild
// use qbrender
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${qbrender.raw_sites(QbStmtSelect(qbbuild.select()))}")
// ```
pub fn raw_sites(s: qbast.QbStmt) -> Int []
    qbast.raw_count(s)

// The SQL this package cannot build, as one line each.
//
// Published as a function rather than left to the README so a caller
// can print it from their own `--help`, and so the list is somewhere a
// test can compare against when it grows.
//
// ```novo
// use std.list
// use qbrender
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${list.len(qbrender.cannot_build())}")
// ```
pub fn cannot_build() -> [Str] []
    [
        "CREATE, ALTER, DROP and GRANT: data definition is an ordered, reversible list of changes rather than a statement",
        "window functions: OVER (PARTITION BY … ORDER BY … ROWS …) is a grammar of its own; splice the SELECT item with QbRaw",
        "GROUPING SETS, CUBE and ROLLUP",
        "VALUES as a table source, LATERAL, TABLESAMPLE and table functions",
        "locking beyond FOR UPDATE: no FOR SHARE, SKIP LOCKED or NOWAIT",
        "vendor hints and comments: the sql-engine dialect reads -- as two minus signs, so a comment there does not parse",
        "type translation: QbCast writes the type name as given, because TEXT is not VARCHAR is not STRING",
        "execution: nothing here opens a connection, and QbRender.text and QbRender.params go to a driver",
        "a device build: a statement is a tree of lists of strings, and a device that talks to a database talks to it over a network"]
